WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.630
Film Courage: Your film BIRDS OF PASSAGE [Pájaros
de verano] is about Colombia.

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:06.850
Why is it so personal to the two of you?

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:08.280
Cristina Gallego, Co-Director: It’s a personal
story.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:17.590
I think even when you’re in a context during
a period film but you are doing a film also

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:23.410
that is set in a community that doesn’t
belong to you.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:29.220
At the same time it speaks about us.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:38.640
This is a family film and we struggle also
with the conflicts of the traditional (a community)

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:46.860
and the modernity that also talk about the
conflict between woman and man and the real

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:52.539
and the spiritual world and this is something
that touches us in a very deep way.

00:00:52.539 --> 00:00:57.059
Film Courage: Ciro I think you said in the
press notes that you wanted to make a movie

00:00:57.059 --> 00:01:04.390
that was not turning criminals into heroes,
showing the horrors of untamed capitalism.

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:08.689
So I know this is a very popular theme but
the way the two of you have shown this film

00:01:08.689 --> 00:01:14.279
exhibits the families and more of the emotional
need behind it (for honor, respect).

00:01:14.279 --> 00:01:19.539
Can you talk about that and why you didn’t
want to turn it into more of one of the popular

00:01:19.539 --> 00:01:21.069
shows that is on currently?

00:01:21.069 --> 00:01:28.591
Ciro Guerra, Co-Director: Yes, we wanted to make
a genre film but this story gave us the opportunity

00:01:28.591 --> 00:01:36.229
to give it a twist to the genre film, like
the gangster film or the noir film that usually

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:42.529
has turned into a glorification of criminals,
a celebration of violence, we wanted to talk

00:01:42.529 --> 00:01:48.689
about this process, what the birth of has
meant in Colombia which is a process of a

00:01:48.689 --> 00:01:54.029
destruction of our cultural roots, a process
of destruction of the basis of society, a

00:01:54.029 --> 00:01:57.989
process of destruction of a family that was
very close-knit.

00:01:57.989 --> 00:02:04.429
And in that process you cannot glorify the
violence that takes place.

00:02:04.429 --> 00:02:10.270
The fact that this story happened within a
society that is matrilineal in which the women

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:16.280
are so strong allowed us to give a twist to
the genre and make it from more of a female

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:26.189
perspective with a female director and strong
female characters and also we decided to every

00:02:26.189 --> 00:02:31.580
element that is usually a celebration of violence
like the action sequences or the excitement

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:36.629
sequences that we were going to keep most
of the violence off-screen.

00:02:36.629 --> 00:02:40.810
And when we kept it on-screen there was not
going to be any way of cutting it to make

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:50.609
it more exciting or more action driven but
it was going to be more about watching the

00:02:50.609 --> 00:02:54.180
way that violence and destruction happens
in front of you with a distance.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:59.060
Film Courage: And I understand Cristina this
is your directorial debut (I know you produced

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.010
many other films).

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:05.260
Was there a story that you can pass along
where something happened on set that didn’t

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:07.379
go as planned and you had to think on your
feet?

00:03:07.379 --> 00:03:10.019
As a first-time director, how was that?

00:03:10.019 --> 00:03:21.349
Cristina: I think for me as a first-time director
everything also was new and that is how I

00:03:21.349 --> 00:03:29.790
like to work, being challenged all the time,
getting out from a place of comfort and this

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:36.390
film has many things and difficulties not
just for me as a first-time director but for

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:38.670
us and for our crew.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:45.319
It was the most challenging shooting that
we have until now.

00:03:45.319 --> 00:03:57.060
And it was just challenging me all the time
to get the concentration to be alive until

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:00.730
the end of the shooting because it was very
hard for us.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:05.599
Film Courage: Lastly I think you’ve talked
about you think faces can tell stories?

00:04:05.599 --> 00:04:09.760
Since I’m not sure if you spoke some of
the actual native language in the film [Wayuu

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:13.420
people with Arawakan language] can you talk
about how you convey that to your actors because

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:15.519
the facial expressions were very real.

00:04:15.519 --> 00:04:16.870
The dialogue didn’t need to be there.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:19.019
You could understand what they meant [without
saying a word].

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:21.350
How did you convey this to your actors and
the viewers?

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:26.750
Ciro: For us authenticity is the most important
part in the process of casting and in the

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:29.010
process of acting.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:33.800
And in this film we have a mix of professional
actors with non-professional actors.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:37.240
And the non-professional actors are from this
region and have are people who have experience

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:42.390
(a life experience) which you can see and
you can feel.

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:47.970
And they were sharing life experience and
also the culture and the language and every

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:55.070
aspect of the Wayuu to the professional actors
who we were working with the and some of them

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:57.720
which were no Wayuu.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:03.460
And also the professional actors were teaching
them the craft of cinema, how to stand on

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:10.680
scene, how to make the dialogue real and so
in this process where they are both learning

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:15.550
from each other is a growing process for both
of them and a growing process for the film

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:22.330
and the result is something that is very transporting
and very authentic in what we mean when we

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:30.020
say that a face has almost like a location,
like the main location of a film is where

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:35.259
the emotion happens and where the audience’s
connection happens.

00:05:35.259 --> 00:05:47.960
Cristina: Yes, I think that as we are different
in skin and are people, each one is talking

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:55.870
not only his own but also about his roots
and that is something that is in the skin

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:59.169
and in the faces of the films.

00:05:59.169 --> 00:06:09.860
As Ciro is saying we are looking for authenticity
not only in the faces but in the language,

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:16.360
in the traditions and in all of the world
that we are creating and this is part of what

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:17.300
we are looking for.

